<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution of Ireland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bunreacht na hÉireann</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution of Ireland, Bunreacht na hÉireann, is the basic law of Ireland, drafted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a> and approved by referendum on 1 July 1937. It replaced the constitution of the Irish Free State, named the state Éire or Ireland, created the office of President and recognised a special position for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, removed in 1972. It has since been changed many times by referendum.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Constitution replaced the Constitution of the Irish Free State, in force since 6 December 1922. There were two main reasons. The Statute of Westminster 1931 made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> sovereign in their own right, and the 1922 text was associated in many eyes with the controversial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a>. Éamon de Valera, leader of the anti-treaty faction, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fianna-fail">Fianna Fáil</a> in 1926, and the party entered government after the 1932 general election.</p><p>De Valera supervised the writing personally. John Hearne, legal adviser to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of External Affairs, drafted it, a group under Micheál Ó Gríobhtha translated it into Irish, and John Charles McQuaid, president of Blackrock College, advised on religious, educational and family questions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a> approved the draft on 14 June 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>It was put to a plebiscite on 1 July 1937, the day of the general election, and passed with 56 percent in favour, 38.6 percent of the whole electorate. Fine Gael and Labour supporters, Unionists and some independents and feminists opposed it. It came into force on 29 December 1937. The text of about 16,000 words opens in the name of the Most Holy Trinity and asserts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the people. It provides for a directly elected, largely ceremonial president, a head of government called the taoiseach, a parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oireachtas">Oireachtas</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a> and Seanad Éireann, and a judiciary headed by the Supreme Court, which can strike down unconstitutional laws.</p><p>Article 8 makes Irish the first official language and English the second, and the Irish text prevails in a conflict. Article 45 sets out directive principles of social policy that no court may enforce. Amendment requires a referendum.</p><p>As adopted, Article 2 claimed the whole island as the national territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> of 1998 led to amended Articles 2 and 3, in force from December 1999, which say a united Ireland may come about only with the consent of majorities in both jurisdictions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On publication the Irish Independent called the draft one of de Valera's finest tributes to his predecessors, while the Irish Times criticised the territorial claim on Northern Ireland and the omission of the British Commonwealth, and the Daily Telegraph objected to the special position of the Roman Catholic Church. The British government protested in a communiqué on 30 December 1937.</p><p>The Fifth Amendment removed the recognition of named denominations and the special position of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. The constitution contained a prohibition of abortion from 1983 to 2018, and the 34th amendment in 2015 provided for same-sex marriage. Referendums in March 2024 rejected proposed changes to the articles on the family and on women's life within the home.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitution of Ireland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Constitution of Ireland</b>, drafted under Eamon de Valera and approved by referendum in 1937, replaced the Irish Free State with a state named Ireland, or Eire, with an elected President, Douglas Hyde being the first.</blockquote><a href="/state/ireland">Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
